WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.140
These two dimensional sheets of
plastic fold themselves

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.460
into 3D shapes when activated by light

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.260
Researchers printed a series of shapes onto
sheets of shrink film.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.380
When exposed to certain wavelengths of light,

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.660
the underlying polymer heats up,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:22.180
causing the plastic to fold
along these “color hinges”.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.860
Flashing types of light in sequence

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:29.340
allow the sheets to fold
into complex shapes.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.060
Red ink to green light,

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.240
cyan ink reacts to
red light,

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.820
and yellow ink to blue light.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.100
The sheets can also be designed to react differently

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.400
to the same wavelength by
using various hues of the same color

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.840
Darker hues react more
to the light more quickly

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.900
Built in “Shape programming” like this
may have the potential

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.300
to be used in implantable medical devices

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.720
smart packaging, or even
in deploying satellites.

